        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Military junta</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1170 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">A committee of senior officers, usually the commanders of the army, navy and air force and sometimes the police, ruling by decree</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">A claim to rescue the nation from disorder, corruption or subversion, resting on control of the armed forces and often on a promise of later elections</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">Officers take power by coup and fill the council by rank; members change through internal manoeuvres or counter-coups, and juntas end by handing over to civilians or being overthrown</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Famous examples</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Goryeo military regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> (1170–1270), the Greek military junta (1967–1974), the Chilean Government Junta (1973–1990), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> (1974–1987), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>’s National Reorganization Process (1976–1983), Myanmar’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-law-and-order-restoration-council">State Law and Order Restoration Council</a> (1988–1997)</span></div>
