    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A military junta is rule by a committee of officers, usually installed by a coup; it differs from a military strongman, who rules alone, and from civilian governments that merely depend on the army.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>’s Goryeo generals ruled collectively from 1170, but juntas became common only after 1945, governing much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Asia and, for shorter spells, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and Portugal.</li><li>Juntas usually promised to restore order and then hold elections; many did hand power back, but many also imprisoned, tortured and killed opponents, and one coup often led to another.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Junta, Spanish for “meeting” or “council”, won its political fame through the committees Spaniards formed in 1808 to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s invasion; these were not military councils, and the word only later came to mean rule by officers.</p></aside>
    </section>

